WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:04.660
SANDEEP PARIKH:
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.030
Thanks for coming.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.070
I know the day's been
running a little bit long,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.550
so I will hopefully
not keep you too long.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.890
Welcome to the session
between you and happy hour.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.931
Hopefully, we'll get you
out of here just in time

00:00:14.931 --> 00:00:16.820
to get some drinks
and get some snacks.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.080
My name is Sandeep Parikh.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.970
I run the cloud
solutions architecture

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.300
team for Americas East,
for Google Cloud Platform,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.790
and today, I want to talk
to you about building

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.310
a collaborative data platform.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.731
Effectively, you've got
teams of individuals spread

00:00:30.731 --> 00:00:32.689
across your company, that
have to work together

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.670
in some meaningful
way, and you need

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.290
them to share data,
talk to each other,

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.940
basically build
something together.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.340
So, how do you enable that?

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.340
So, we're going to walk
through some of the ways

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.580
that we tell a lot of customers,
and developers, and partners

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.800
how to do that stuff
in the real world.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.620
First, we're going to
meet our hypothetical team

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.390
of individuals, and
we're going to learn

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.790
about the tasks that have to
do, and the tools that they

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.440
typically use.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.330
And then, we'll
jump into mapping

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.240
the tools they use to the tools
available in Google Cloud,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.890
so you can start to
see how they might

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.166
get a consistent set of
tools and work together.

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:07.790
And then, we're going
to talk about how

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.360
we enable collaboration.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.110
This is how we do
things, like set up teams

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:16.010
to do self-service and
easy data discovery.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.260
I'm probably going to
trivialize a handful of details,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.649
but stay with me, it'll make
sense as we get through it.

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:23.690
And then, I want to cover
what the new workflow's

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.970
like once you get all
this stuff up and running,

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.636
and then give you a
couple little things

00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:29.520
and tips on how to get started.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.550
Does that make
sense to everybody?

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.110
Head nods?

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.030
OK, good.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.890
All right, so let's
meet the team.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.380
All right, so first, we've
got about four individuals.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.530
We've got a data scientist,
a data engineer, a business

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.050
analyst, and an app developer.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.850
Ultimately, they all have
three things in common.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.810
They use different data
sets, those data sets

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.560
are all in different
formats, and they all

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.730
use different tools, right?

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.620
So, there's not a
ton of overlap here,

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.020
and that's kind
of the challenge.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.700
So, let's assume for a second,
or let's imagine for a second,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.520
we've got this
team, and their job

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.340
is to build a recommendation
engine for a retail site.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.765
So, what are the kinds of things
that each person on the team

00:02:07.765 --> 00:02:09.139
is going to need
to be successful

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:10.187
in order to do their job?

00:02:10.187 --> 00:02:12.020
So, the first thing
we'll cover is the data,

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.610
and then we'll talk
about the tools.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.940
So, from a data perspective,
Alice, the data scientist,

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.880
needs kind of cleansed,
normalized data.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.670
Things that have no
longer log files, right,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.620
not just raw rows
from a database,

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.150
but things that
actually make sense.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.040
And those could be,
for example, stuff

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.240
like purchase history, product
history, product metadata,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.030
click streams, product reviews.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.390
All the stuff that it would
take to actually craft and model

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.380
a recommendation problem.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:44.020
Then, we've got Bob,
the data engineer.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.390
He's going to have to
take all of the raw data

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.827
and turn it into
something useful.

00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:49.660
So, he's probably going
to need things like,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.220
logs information, product
transactions or purchase

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.820
history, product
metadata, as well.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.590
This is all the stuff
that's coming in

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.929
straight off the
application, that he's

00:02:58.929 --> 00:03:00.970
got to turn into something
useful for other parts

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.710
of the organization.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:04.700
Then, there's Ned.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.320
Ned is the business
analyst, and he

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.330
needs a lot of
aggregated data, things

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.070
that he can use to
generate statistics

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.680
and understanding about how
the business is performing.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.290
Not just, again, rows
or log files, but again,

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.270
that next level up from,
sort of, the basics.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.340
And then, finally, we have
Jan, the app developer.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.879
She's going to need users
and product predictions,

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:26.670
or recommendations.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.211
She's going to be
able to-- she needs

00:03:28.211 --> 00:03:30.390
to be able to build
new microservices that

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.580
expose things like this
recommendation engine

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.660
to the end user.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.660
So, that can be things
like a recommendations API,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.200
access to wish lists, some
knowledge around similar users

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.990
or similar products, how
things complement each other.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.480
But, you can see there's a
little bit of overlap here,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.356
but not a ton, right.

00:03:46.356 --> 00:03:47.730
They all need
something different

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.980
and they kind of need it in
a slightly different format.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.810
Now, if you think
about sort of a--

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.100
I tried to draw a
sequence diagram here,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.050
and it got a little
complicated, so I simplified it

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:56.591
a little bit.

00:03:56.591 --> 00:03:59.350
But, ultimately, Alice and
Bob have to agree and talk

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:00.050
to each other.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.091
Bob and Ned have to agree
and talk to each other.

00:04:02.091 --> 00:04:03.730
Ned and Alice have
to talk each other,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.604
and Jan and Alice have
to talk to each other.

00:04:05.604 --> 00:04:07.750
You know, there's a lot of
communication happening.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.546
And, listen, I love talking
to my coworkers just as much

00:04:10.546 --> 00:04:11.920
as the next person,
but if you're

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.670
spending all of your time trying
to explain to them what you

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:17.709
need, when you need
it, where it came from,

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:20.260
what it should look like, it
becomes challenging, right.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.060
It slows down the
ability for them

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.530
to make progress
and work quickly.

00:04:26.525 --> 00:04:28.150
Ultimately, this is
the problem that we

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.480
see a lot of folks having
out in the, world, right?

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.950
They each have to
find data sets.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:34.690
They each have to
figure out who owns it.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.280
They have to figure out
how things are related

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.863
to each other, and they have to
manage all that by themselves,

00:04:38.863 --> 00:04:42.190
which, again, takes time away
from their ultimate task.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:46.240
Just by a show of hands, does
this sound familiar to anybody?

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.720
Oh, that's a scary
number, actually.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.514
Maybe I shouldn't have
asked that question.

00:04:50.514 --> 00:04:51.930
No, but that this
is great, right?

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.330
This is how we have to-- we
have to find, understand,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.320
what the problem is, then
try to solve it together.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.990
All right, so let's
talk about tools.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:04:59.920
That's the first step.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.461
We need to understand
what everyone's

00:05:01.461 --> 00:05:03.820
working with in order to
kind of make the next jump.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.030
So, Alice is the data
scientist, huge fan of Python,

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:07.849
like myself.

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:09.640
It's like her Swiss
army knife, she uses it

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:10.960
for just about everything.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.730
Typically, does
a lot of her work

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.190
with IPython or
Jupyter notebooks,

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.430
pulls data down, does
it on her workstation,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:20.620
you know, slices
dices data, uses

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.720
a lot of pretty common
data science frameworks,

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.540
uses things like, NumPy,
SciPy, scikit-learn,

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.350
Pandas, because
she has a history,

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.500
and/or likes data frames.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.160
That's a typical approach, from
a data science perspective.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.110
Bob is a huge fan
of Java, right?

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.960
He does lots of big
ETL pipeline work,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.590
does a lot of orchestrating
data from one format to another,

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.960
or multiple sources of data
together into something

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.860
that looks cohesive
on the other end.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:49.390
So, he's typically using
tools like MapReduce or Spark.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.880
In some cases, he might be
using Apache Beam, which

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.296
you'll actually hear more about
over the next couple days,

00:05:54.296 --> 00:05:56.850
as well.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.770
Ned is like a spreadsheet guy.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.710
He loves SQL and he
loves spreadsheets.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.850
So, what he likes to do
is build custom reports

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.810
and dashboards, very SQL-driven,
got to have some kind of access

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.730
to a data warehouse,
so he can do giant--

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.280
you know, kind of cube
rotations and understanding.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.040
Basically, he's got to be
able to prove to the business

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.820
how products are performing,
why things like a recommendation

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.470
engine is even necessary.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:23.869
So, it's his responsibility
to kind of facilitate that.

00:06:23.869 --> 00:06:25.660
And then, we've got
Jan, the app developer.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.970
Jan's a typical kind of
polyglot app developer.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.760
Likes writing stuff on
microservices, likes delivering

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.850
features very quickly, you know,
may have a language preference

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:36.670
one way or the other, but has to
maintain a lot of the existing

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:37.344
infrastructure.

00:06:37.344 --> 00:06:39.010
So, that could be
things like node apps.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:43.030
That could be simple things like
Python apps with Flask or Ruby

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:44.470
apps with Sinatra,
all the way up

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.724
to like kitchen sink frameworks,
like Django or Rails.

00:06:47.724 --> 00:06:49.640
Just depends, right,
whatever the use case is.

00:06:52.147 --> 00:06:53.230
So, we've gotten that far.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.270
So, we understand
their task, we know

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.460
who all the key players
are, and we know the things

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:58.760
that they like to use.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.580
So, the next step
for us is, how do we

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.080
figure out which
tools they should

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.312
be using in Google Cloud?

00:07:05.312 --> 00:07:07.110
All right, we can
skip that slide.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.000
So, what we'll do
is let's lay out

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.751
a handful of the tools
that are available.

00:07:10.751 --> 00:07:12.666
And we're not going to
cover everything that's

00:07:12.666 --> 00:07:14.860
in GCP right now, because
we don't have enough time,

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.110
and I want you guys to have
snacks at some point soon.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.100
So, what we'll do is kind of
focus on the core set of things

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.470
that are critical for each
of those individual roles

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.060
to have access to.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.550
So, the first part, you know,
for things like applications,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:27.720
we've got Compute.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.730
So, with things
like virtual machine

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.100
or virtual instances
with Compute Engine,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.590
manage Kubernetes clusters
with Container Engine,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.510
or just kind of manage
container deployments,

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.780
or Docker container
deployments with App Engine.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.920
It's a good place to start.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.620
On the storage front,
cloud storage for objects,

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.160
Cloud SQL for relational data,
Cloud Datastore for, you know,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.770
NoSQL, or
non-relational datasets,

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.900
and Cloud Bigtable
for wide column.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.400
And then, on the
data and analytics,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.470
are then the more
processing kind of side,

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:00.140
Dataproc for managed
Hadoop and Spark clusters,

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.770
Datalab for IPython
notebooks, BigQuery for kind

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.070
of an analytical data warehouse,
and data flow for running

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:10.712
managed Beam, Apache
Beam pipelines.

00:08:10.712 --> 00:08:12.920
And then, the last column,
or the last kind of group,

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:14.280
is machine learning.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.980
So we've got cloud
machine learning,

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:18.020
which is kind of running
TensorFlow models,

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:19.850
and running them
at scale, things

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.850
like the natural language API,
the speech API, or the vision

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:24.470
API.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:25.977
As you start to
think about the team

00:08:25.977 --> 00:08:27.560
that we just introduced,
and you start

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.500
looking at this sort of bucket
of tools, of this set of puzzle

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.330
pieces, you can start
to see where things

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.925
are going to kind
of fit together

00:08:33.925 --> 00:08:35.299
There's a consistent
set of tools

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.770
here that we can map
back on to the team.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.980
So let's walk through each
one of those individually,

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:42.710
really quickly.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.680
So, for data science workloads,
Alice has a couple of options.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.180
We already talked about the
fact that she's a fan of Python,

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.510
so that maps really well.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.070
So, with Python,
she has two options.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:52.930
She can either
use Cloud Datalab,

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:55.330
or she can use Cloud
Dataproc with Jupyter.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:58.180
With Datalab, what she gets
is a complete Python dev

00:08:58.180 --> 00:08:59.500
environment.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.010
You know, it's bundled in with
like NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.770
so she can kind of kick off her
work, and build those charts,

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.395
and build those, kind
of understanding,

00:09:07.395 --> 00:09:08.950
or on the data set as she would.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.450
Additionally, on
top of that though,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.330
it's also got built-in support
for TensorFlow and BigQuery.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:15.940
This means she's got a complete
environment to go and start

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.590
maybe prototyping
models that she wants

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:18.850
to build with
TensorFlow, if she's

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.950
trying to build a very
compelling recommendation

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.000
mechanism.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.920
Or, if she needs data
that lives in BigQuery,

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.710
she can actually do inline SQL
statements there, and pull data

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.690
back out, or offload queries
to BigQuery, as well.

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.450
So, she's got a handful
of options there.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:34.540
The nice thing about Datalab is
that it's based on Jupyter, so,

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.040
it's got a little
bit of background.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.290
So, it should look
very familiar to her.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:42.850
But it is somewhat
constrained onto just Python.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.890
If she's got more
specific needs,

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.512
or wants to include additional
frameworks or kernels,

00:09:47.512 --> 00:09:49.720
then we have to look at
something like Cloud Dataproc

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.040
plus Jupyter.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.830
So, what you can do is you
can spin up a Cloud Dataproc

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:54.790
cluster--

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.490
again, a managed YARN
cluster, effectively--

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.160
and then have Jupyter
pre-installed on there

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.280
and ready to go.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:04.905
So, it takes about 90 seconds
to fire up a Hadoop cluster,

00:10:04.905 --> 00:10:06.280
anywhere from like
three, to like

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:07.750
a couple of thousand nodes.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.291
In this case, for Alice, I think
just three nodes is probably

00:10:10.291 --> 00:10:13.000
appropriate, and her goal is
to just spin this up and get

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.284
Jupyter pre-installed.

00:10:14.284 --> 00:10:15.700
Once she's got
Jupyter, then she's

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.140
back to the exact
same environment

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:21.310
she had on her laptop, support
for over 80 different languages

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.077
and frameworks or kernels.

00:10:23.077 --> 00:10:24.910
But, the nice thing
also is built-in support

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:27.370
for PySpark and Spark MLlib.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:29.350
So, you know, if
you're kind of trying

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:33.116
to figure out where the machine
learning line sort of falls,

00:10:33.116 --> 00:10:34.990
there's definitely a
handful of more sessions

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:37.990
you can attend around things
like TensorFlow or on Dataproc.

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:39.850
What I would urge you
to do is, if you've

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.250
got kind of an individual like
this in your organization,

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:44.230
is have them explore both.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:45.930
TensorFlow might be appropriate.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.005
Spark MLlib might
be appropriate.

00:10:48.005 --> 00:10:49.630
And there should be
a clean distinction

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:50.879
between what they can each do.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.780
All right, on the data
processing front, same thing.

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:58.470
Bob's got a couple
of different options.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:01.590
He can either use Cloud Dataproc
or he can use Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.960
With Dataproc, again, we just
talked about this briefly,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.650
but managed Hadoop
and Spark clusters,

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:08.566
the nice thing about Dataproc--

00:11:08.566 --> 00:11:10.440
and I'm only going to
spend a minute on this,

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.520
so, by all means, if you're
interested we can do questions

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.980
afterwards-- but the
nice thing about Dataproc

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.220
is that it turns that kind of
mindset of a Hadoop cluster

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:21.920
or a YARN cluster, on its head.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:25.181
You end up going away from
having a cluster-centric view

00:11:25.181 --> 00:11:27.680
of the world, and turning it
into more of a job-centric view

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:28.650
of the world.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.490
So instead of firing up
a thousand-node cluster

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.800
that everybody
shares, you could have

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:37.530
every individual job have their
own thousand-node cluster.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.432
And as long as those jobs can
be done in less than 24 hours,

00:11:40.432 --> 00:11:42.140
you can actually take
advantage of things

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:45.560
like preemptible OVMs or
preemptible instances.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:48.860
Those instances are
about 80% off list price.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.525
So, you get the ability to do
this job dramatically faster

00:11:51.525 --> 00:11:53.900
than ever before, because you
have all this extra compute

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.180
sitting around, and you get
to do it really cheaply,

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.910
because it only takes
a few hours anyway.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:00.860
So Bob's got that option.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.470
He can also do things like
tune the cluster parameters,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.540
or if he's got custom
JAR files he needs,

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.660
easily bootstrap those across
the cluster, doesn't matter.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.290
So, h has that option.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.200
The other approach
is to use Dataflow.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.300
Dataflow is a managed
service that we

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:16.810
have for running
Apache Beam workloads.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:20.540
Apache Beam is basically
a programming model,

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:22.520
or an approach, that
unifies batch and stream

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.440
processing into one.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.450
When you take Beam
workloads or Beam pipelines

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:30.840
and you push them to Cloud
Dataflow, we run those for you

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.772
in a totally managed, kind of
automatically scalable fashion.

00:12:34.772 --> 00:12:35.730
So, that's pretty nice.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.730
And Apache Beam workload's
on Cloud Dataflow,

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.350
actually support
templates for kind

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.780
of like easy
parameterization and staging

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:44.520
and things like that.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.250
The way I kind think about the
relationship here of which path

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.440
you go down is really
a question of what

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.240
do you have existing already.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:54.930
If you've got a huge investment
in Spark or MapReduce jobs,

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.810
or just kind of like oozy
workflows around those things,

00:12:57.810 --> 00:12:59.940
by all means, go down
to Cloud Dataproc group.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.327
It's a turnkey solution.

00:13:01.327 --> 00:13:03.660
You can take your job and
just push it to a new cluster,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:04.950
just like that.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.260
If it's net new and you're
starting from scratch,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.660
I think Beam is a good
approach to look at.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.919
It's relatively new, so
it's a little bit young.

00:13:11.919 --> 00:13:14.460
It's definitely not as mature
as some of the other components

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.120
in the Hadoop ecosystem.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.860
But, it does have
this really, really,

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.820
really critical advantage where
you can take a batch pipeline

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:22.380
and turn it into a
streaming pipeline

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.787
just by changing a
couple of lines of input.

00:13:24.787 --> 00:13:26.370
So Bob's got a couple
of options here,

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:29.400
to kind of match up to what he
typically likes to work with.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.710
All right, Ned's use case
is actually really simple.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:33.000
He needs a data warehouse.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.050
He needs a data warehouse
that supports SQL,

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:37.430
and that can scale with
whatever size of data he needs,

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.570
or that he's got
access to, and he's

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.060
got to be able to plug it
into a whole host of tools,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.410
kind of downstream.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.720
So, BigQuery's a
great fit for him,

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.200
enterprise cloud
analytical data warehouse.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:50.664
It's a fully managed mechanism.

00:13:50.664 --> 00:13:52.080
We often use the
word server-less,

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.380
though I hate that
term, so I apologize.

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:57.600
It supports standard
SQL and it does scale up

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.070
to a kind of petabyte scale.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.260
So, he has the option of
running something that's

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.530
anywhere from data sets that are
about gigabytes all the way up

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:08.604
to petabytes, and still get
responses back within seconds.

00:14:08.604 --> 00:14:10.020
BigQuery is great,
because it does

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.405
support kind of batch loading,
or streaming inserts, as well.

00:14:13.405 --> 00:14:15.780
And it's got built in things
like security and durability

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.250
and automatic availability,
and all the other good stuff.

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:21.880
But, ultimately, the best part
is that Ned gets to use SQL,

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:24.120
gets to query really,
really large data sets,

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.020
and visualize and explore
the data as he's used to,

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:30.210
with the typical tools
and expertise he's got.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.820
When it comes time to create
reports and dashboards

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.310
and things like that, there's
a couple of options he has.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:36.780
One, he can use
Data Studio, which

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.380
is built into the platform.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:40.950
So, it's kind of like a Google
Doc style approach, where

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:42.777
I can create a new
report, and then I

00:14:42.777 --> 00:14:44.610
can share that with
everybody in the company

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.694
without actually having
to create multiple copies.

00:14:46.694 --> 00:14:48.443
And people can edit
things like parameters

00:14:48.443 --> 00:14:50.230
and watch the difference
in those reports,

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:51.146
and see how they look.

00:14:51.146 --> 00:14:52.680
But effectively,
he has the ability

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.864
to create those
reports and dashboards.

00:14:54.864 --> 00:14:57.280
Alternatively, he could use
things like Tableau or Looker,

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:58.890
or other business
intelligence tools

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:00.300
that he's that he's a fan of.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.559
So, he's got a handful
of options there.

00:15:02.559 --> 00:15:05.100
And the nice thing also is that
because of all this approach,

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.860
like BigQuery also
supports other kind of JDBC

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.680
infrastructure, so a lot of the
tooling that Ned is typically

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.150
used to can plug right
into BigQuery, as well.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:16.612
So, the last one is Jan.

00:15:16.612 --> 00:15:17.820
We talked about this earlier.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.750
Jan likes to deploy and
scale microservices,

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:22.610
so she's got two
easy options there.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.550
If she's really, really
focused on complex container

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:26.870
orchestration, writes them--

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.020
wants to deploy things that way,
Container Engine's a great fit.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.690
It's based on open
sourced Kubernetes.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.620
We've got built-in health
checking, monitoring, logging.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:36.754
We've got a private
Container Registry,

00:15:36.754 --> 00:15:38.420
or she can go down
the app engine route,

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:40.670
and just take her Docker
containers, and push them up,

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:42.290
and we'll auto-scale
them for her.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:45.410
The good thing is that along
with kind of the same health

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.600
checking, monitoring,
and logging,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.150
App Engine also includes
a built-in load balancer

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:51.830
up front, has
things like version

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:54.530
management, traffic
splitting, as well,

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:56.270
and automatic security scanning.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.920
So, again, a couple
of options here

00:15:57.920 --> 00:15:59.253
that kind of make sense for her.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:03.020
So, that's right.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:03.646
We're all done.

00:16:03.646 --> 00:16:05.353
Everybody's got tools,
they can all work,

00:16:05.353 --> 00:16:06.430
and everybody is off--

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:08.420
off to the races.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.760
That's not true.

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.856
We've only just kind of
scratched the surface.

00:16:11.856 --> 00:16:14.230
So, they all have a consistent
set of tools to work with,

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:17.350
but we actually haven't enabled
any of the collaboration bits

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:17.980
yet.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.166
So, we still have to
figure out how to get them,

00:16:20.166 --> 00:16:21.790
not only to work
together, but actually

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:24.230
to work in a way that
makes sense, and scales up.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:24.730
Right.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.230
Their team is four people today.

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:29.800
It could be eight, 12, 16,
you know, in a few weeks.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.900
So, we've got to
come up with a better

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:36.820
approach to managing the data
that they need access to.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:39.870
So, if you're trying to
enable collaboration,

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:41.700
there are a handful of
things you're really

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:42.820
going to think about.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:45.030
The first is, you've
got to find consistency.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:48.870
And it's not just consistency
in the tool set, right?

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.540
That's also important, but
you also need consistency

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:54.480
in terms of where do you
expect data to be coming from,

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.020
where do you expect data to
be used downstream, right?

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.020
That's really important.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.436
If you can't agree
on the sources,

00:16:59.436 --> 00:17:02.760
if you can't agree on what
the downstream workloads are,

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.099
it's hard to really
understand how

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:07.950
everybody should work together
and use the same set of tools.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.130
The next thing you want to do,
is take a really good hard look

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:13.470
at all the data that you're
trying to get access to.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:14.579
And this isn't true
for every piece

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:16.290
of data that lives in
your organization, right?

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:17.748
It might be things
that are focused

00:17:17.748 --> 00:17:19.890
on certain sets of the
company, like certain teams,

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:22.530
or certain tasks, but
ultimately, you've

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.030
got to figure out where all
this data lives, and take

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.114
an inventory on it, right?

00:17:26.114 --> 00:17:27.488
Take an inventory
on it, and make

00:17:27.488 --> 00:17:29.790
sure it's in the right
storage medium for everyone

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.600
to use as time goes on.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.640
The next thing you
want to do is come up

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:37.190
with an approach
around metadata, right?

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.110
This is pretty
simple, but if you're

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.360
trying to figure out
who owns a piece of data

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:45.860
or where it came from, what
other data sets it's related

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:48.650
to, what are some of the
types of data that are located

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.220
in this data set without
actually having to go query it,

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:52.970
that's a really
challenging problem to do,

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:56.390
when you think about having
hundreds or thousands

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:58.130
of individual pieces
of data spread out

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.390
across your infrastructure.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:03.870
Then, you want to enable
discovery and self-service.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.600
You want people to be able to go
find these things by themselves

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.450
and pull them down as
needed, without having to go,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.190
again, spend all
that time arguing

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.695
with people about
formats, and availability,

00:18:13.695 --> 00:18:16.320
and tooling, and worrying about
where you're going to store it,

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:17.740
as well.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:19.320
And then the last
thing is security,

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.970
right Don't leave
that on the table.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.345
It's certainly important to
understand security, and more

00:18:23.345 --> 00:18:25.910
broadly, identity, right, to
make sure we're tracking access

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.350
to all these things.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.270
All right, so how do
we start with this?

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:33.230
I throw this in there.

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:35.720
You have to kind of embrace
the concept of a data lake.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:37.680
I'm not suggesting you
have to go build one.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:40.620
I know it's a totally
loaded buzzword and term,

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:41.785
but you have to build--

00:18:41.785 --> 00:18:44.160
you have to embrace the idea
of it, on some level, right?

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:45.730
And this is kind of
what I said earlier--

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.580
if you start at the
very bottom, you first

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.440
have to understand what are all
the sources of data I've got,

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:50.940
right?

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.731
At least get everybody
to agree in the room

00:18:52.731 --> 00:18:55.170
where data is coming in
from, and what it looks like.

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:58.470
Once you do that, you want to
find some, again, consistency

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.645
on which tools are we
going to use to store data?

00:19:00.645 --> 00:19:02.520
You're not going to
consume every single part

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:03.682
of the platform, right?

00:19:03.682 --> 00:19:05.640
It doesn't make sense to
just use every service

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:06.455
because it's there.

00:19:06.455 --> 00:19:08.580
Find the ones that make
the most sense for the data

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:09.246
that you've got.

00:19:09.246 --> 00:19:11.202
And we'll go through
that a second, as well.

00:19:11.202 --> 00:19:13.410
And the last thing is, what
are the use cases, right?

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:17.690
How is the data going to
get used over time, right?

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.790
It's important that you
understand what those use

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.290
cases are, because they're going
to drive back to the data--

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:23.420
or to the storage mediums.

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:25.190
And it's important to
pick the storage mediums,

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.064
because that's going to
depend pretty heavily

00:19:27.064 --> 00:19:29.030
on how the data comes
in, where it comes from,

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:32.280
and what it looks like.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:34.280
All right, so where
should data live, right?

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:35.800
We know the sources,
we know the workloads,

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:36.970
but what do we do
in the middle there?

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:38.930
How do we figure out
which places to put data?

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.680
So this is kind of a
simple decision tree.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.240
And I'll go into a little bit
more depth on the next slide

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:43.740
as well.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.150
But some of this you can
kind of simplify, right?

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:48.940
If it's really
structured data, you're

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.170
kind of down to two options.

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:53.440
Is it sort of OLTP data, or
is that OLAP data, right?

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.580
Is it transactional,
or is that analytical?

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:58.930
If it's transactional, Cloud
SQL's a great fit, right?

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.690
It's a typical relational
database, no frills,

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:03.709
does what it does, and
it does it really well.

00:20:03.709 --> 00:20:05.750
On the analytical side,
you have BigQuery, right?

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:07.270
So it depends on
what the use case

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:09.640
is that's starting to drive
it-- not just the structure,

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:11.770
but with the use cases.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.440
The next column
is semi-structure.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.940
So, you've got something that
you might know the schema for,

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:17.200
but it could change.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:18.910
It could adapt in flight.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:21.700
People might be
deploying new mobile apps

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:23.500
somewhere that customers
are going to use.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:25.240
They're going to
start capturing data

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:26.650
they weren't capturing before.

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:28.280
All those things are possible.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.564
So, if you've got
semi-structured data,

00:20:30.564 --> 00:20:32.980
then it's a question of, how
do I need to query that data?

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:35.935
Again, we're back to what
is the downstream use case?

00:20:35.935 --> 00:20:38.560
If it's something where you need
to query in any possible field

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:41.050
that you write in, data
source is a good choice.

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:44.170
When you write a piece of
JSON to Cloud Datastore,

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:46.780
we automatically index
every key by default.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:47.830
That's part of that JSON.

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:48.970
Now, you can turn that
off, if you want to,

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:50.950
or you can pick the keys you
want, but, ultimately, you

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:52.562
have the ability
to query anything

00:20:52.562 --> 00:20:53.770
that you've written in there.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.380
Whereas when you put
data in a Bigtable,

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:58.630
you're basically stuck
with trying to have

00:20:58.630 --> 00:20:59.680
to query by the row key.

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:01.138
And that's actually
pretty powerful

00:21:01.138 --> 00:21:03.020
for a lot of really
great use cases,

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:07.316
especially like time series
data or transactional data.

00:21:07.316 --> 00:21:08.940
But it's not a great
fit, again, if you

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:10.680
want to try to query
some random column,

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.520
you know, somewhere buried
throughout the dataset.

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.981
And the last one is
object, images media,

00:21:15.981 --> 00:21:18.480
that sort of thing, that just
go straight into cloud storage

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:21.247
so, it's a pretty
simple approach.

00:21:21.247 --> 00:21:22.830
So, if you break
this out a little bit

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.582
and start getting in
a few more examples,

00:21:24.582 --> 00:21:26.790
you kind of end up with a
chart that looks like this.

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:29.250
We just covered object storage
really quickly a second

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:32.370
ago great for binary data,
great for object data,

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.870
media, backups,
that sort of thing.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:37.500
On the non-relational
side for Cloud Datastore,

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.510
it's really good for
hierarchical data.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.490
Again, think of like
JSON as a good example

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.824
there, to fit in
there, obviously,

00:21:43.824 --> 00:21:45.240
great on like
mobile applications,

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.710
that sort of thing.

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.410
On the Bigtable side, really,
really powerful system

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.340
for heavy reads and writes,
but you have the row key only,

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:54.174
as your only option.

00:21:54.174 --> 00:21:55.548
There's a little
bit of filtering

00:21:55.548 --> 00:21:57.030
you can apply
outbound on a query,

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:59.190
but really, it's
driven by the row key.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.050
So you've got a very
specific workload

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:04.230
you can use at a Bigtable
on the relational side you

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:05.190
have two options.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.750
And I mentioned-- and I
didn't mention Spanner yet,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:08.400
and it's still a little
bit early on Spanner,

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.483
but I did want to put it
into context for kind of,

00:22:10.483 --> 00:22:12.720
for everybody in the room.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.400
Cloud SQL's great
for web frameworks.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.566
Typical web applications
that you're going to build,

00:22:16.566 --> 00:22:18.852
typical CRED
applications are great.

00:22:18.852 --> 00:22:20.060
Spanner's really interesting.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.360
It's new for us.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:22.830
It's still early.

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.330
Spanner hasn't gone into
general availability quite yet,

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:27.330
but it's an interesting
thing to keep an eye on,

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:28.913
especially as people
are building kind

00:22:28.913 --> 00:22:31.740
of global-facing applications,
where their customers could be

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:35.250
just about anywhere, and
having a mechanism that

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:38.130
has a globally distributed
SQL infrastructure is really

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:38.980
powerful.

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.396
So, it's something to keep
your eye on as you kind of make

00:22:41.396 --> 00:22:43.590
more progress with GCP.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.469
And the last one
is warehouse data.

00:22:45.469 --> 00:22:48.010
Data warehouse, BigQuery, that's
the right place to put that.

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:53.210
So, this is where
it gets interesting.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:56.250
You found the tools,
you found the sources,

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:57.780
and you found the workloads.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:01.170
How do you continue on this
path of enabling self-service

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:02.400
and discovery?

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.890
So, you've taken
all the data sets.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.584
We've taken inventory
of all of them.

00:23:06.584 --> 00:23:08.250
They're all located
in the right places,

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:09.570
but there might be a lot of it.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.430
So how do we enable
people to actually find

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:13.180
the things they're
looking for over time?

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.300
And this is where metadata
is really important,

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.990
because I think if you guys can
kind of guess where I'm going,

00:23:17.990 --> 00:23:18.780
ultimately, what
we're going to do

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:20.320
is we're going to try
to build a catalog,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.736
and that catalog is what's
going to drive everyone's usage

00:23:22.736 --> 00:23:25.834
downstream, and everyone's
ability to work on their own.

00:23:25.834 --> 00:23:27.250
So, in order to
build the catalog,

00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:29.310
though, you've got
to agree on metadata.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.410
And actually, as it
turns out, fortuitously,

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:34.410
or fortunately I should say, the
Google research team actually

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.420
just published an
interesting blog post

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.580
about facilitating discovery
of public data sets.

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:43.560
And in it they cover things
like ownership, provenance,

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:45.000
the type of data
that's located--

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.950
that's contained
within the data set.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:50.220
They relationships
between various data sets,

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.100
can you get consistent
representations,

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:55.480
can you standardize some
of the descriptive tools

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:57.210
that we use to do it.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:58.626
It's basically just JSON.

00:23:58.626 --> 00:24:00.000
There's nothing
fancy about this,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.300
but it forces everyone
to come up and say,

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:05.250
I have a consistent
representation

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:06.910
of every single data set.

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:09.420
So, you start to imagine, if
you have 100 data sets strewn

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.840
across the company, you're
only saying to everybody,

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:14.610
if you are an owner
of a data set,

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:16.914
just publish this
small little JSON file,

00:24:16.914 --> 00:24:18.330
and hand it over
to us, and now we

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:20.520
can start cataloging this data.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:21.630
That's really powerful.

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:24.180
What's even better is, if
you've got that JSON data,

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:25.760
we already had a
place to put it.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.010
So, you have two options here.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:30.210
You can either push that JSON
data into Cloud Datastore

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:32.030
or into BigQuery.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:34.320
And I'll kind of cover
the difference here.

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.450
Cloud Datastores are a
great place for this data,

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:37.560
for a variety of reasons.

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:39.360
Again, JSON's really
well represented.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:42.019
You can query about any
possible field there.

00:24:42.019 --> 00:24:44.310
But the downside is, that
Datastore doesn't really have

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:46.470
kind of a good user-facing UI.

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:48.426
It's very much
application-centric.

00:24:48.426 --> 00:24:50.550
So, if you want to go ahead
and build a little CRED

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:54.150
API on top of this thing,
Datastore can be a good fit.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:56.850
The other option is,
frankly BigQuery.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.187
You know, same idea around
Datastore and storing JSON,

00:24:59.187 --> 00:25:01.770
in fact, this is an example of--
this screenshot as an example

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:03.720
of what this JSON
looks like in BigQuery,

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:05.864
because it does
support nested columns.

00:25:05.864 --> 00:25:08.280
BigQuery is great because,
you've got a UI attached to it.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.363
There's a console right
there, so you can actually

00:25:10.363 --> 00:25:11.670
run SQL queries on it.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:13.935
SQL's relatively universal
across a lot of folks.

00:25:13.935 --> 00:25:15.310
They understand
it really easily.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:18.150
So, this might make a
little bit more sense.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:20.820
To be totally fair and
totally honest with you guys,

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:22.530
when I typically
recommend this approach,

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:24.480
I often will push
people to BigQuery,

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.402
as a place to build this
data catalog around,

00:25:26.402 --> 00:25:28.110
because it just makes
sense, and it plugs

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.000
into a lot of downstream tools.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.740
Datastore can make
sense, but it's

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:34.260
very, very particularly
dependent on how

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:35.807
the team wants to work.

00:25:35.807 --> 00:25:37.890
For trying to find kind
of a good generic solution

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:39.690
to start with,
BigQuery's a great fit.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:44.480
So, once you've done
that, you've pushed--

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:47.320
we've inventoried, we've
cataloged, we've got metadata,

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:49.730
and we've got everything
living inside of BigQuery now.

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:51.907
So, how does this
change the workflow?

00:25:51.907 --> 00:25:53.990
I'm only going to pick on
one, because they're all

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:56.031
going to start looking a
little bit similar, if I

00:25:56.031 --> 00:25:57.371
keep eating through it.

00:25:57.371 --> 00:25:59.370
But, let's talk about the
data science workflow.

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:03.500
So, for Alice, now instead
of having to go talk to Bob,

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.240
or go talk to Ned,
or go talk to Jan,

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:06.823
or talk to anybody
else in the company

00:26:06.823 --> 00:26:09.410
about what data is out
there that she can use,

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:12.290
the first thing she can do is
start to query the catalog.

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:14.900
So, she can just drop
into the BigQuery UI,

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:18.020
and run a simple SQL query,
and explore the data sets

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:19.819
that she has access to.

00:26:19.819 --> 00:26:21.860
The next thing she can
do, because part of what's

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:24.560
in there, one of the pieces of
metadata, is what is the URL,

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:26.460
or what is the download
location for this,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:29.180
she can go ahead and pull that
data into her environment.

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:31.040
Again, whether she's
running a Cloud Datalab

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:32.870
notebook, or a
Jupyter notebook, she

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:34.760
can pull that data
into her environment.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:37.970
And then, she can start to
prototype a TensorFlow model,

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:38.470
for example.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.678
She could start building a
little bit of a TensorFlow

00:26:40.678 --> 00:26:42.362
model and running
some early analysis

00:26:42.362 --> 00:26:44.570
of measuring how well her
recommendation engine might

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:45.830
be working.

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:48.920
Once she does that, she might
have created some new datasets.

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:51.350
She might have actually
taken something and said,

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.180
now I actually have
training data and test

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:54.720
data to work against.

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:56.570
So, now that she's
created two new datasets,

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.470
the next thing she's going to do
is upload those to the catalog.

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:01.379
Write the metadata,
push that in,

00:27:01.379 --> 00:27:03.920
so now other people that might
want to build this stuff later

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:06.200
on, have access to the
same training and testing

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:07.440
that she used.

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:11.450
So, we're starting to create
this kind of lifecycle around.

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.820
Making sure that if you create
something new, make sure

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:16.235
it gets shared with everybody
as quickly as possible.

00:27:16.235 --> 00:27:18.110
And then, she's going
to continue on her work

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:19.130
like she normally does.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:20.900
She'll train her
machine-learning models.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:22.430
She might push that
TensorFlow model

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:23.990
to, like, the cloud ML service.

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:27.170
And then, because the cloud
ML service that she created

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.810
is actually a data
kind of a resource,

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:33.780
she can actually create a
catalog entry for that service.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.470
So, now if someone says, I
want to find a recommendation

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:39.137
service within the
catalog, as long as she's

00:27:39.137 --> 00:27:40.970
tagged and labeled
everything appropriately,

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:44.370
they could find the URL for
that service immediately.

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:46.137
And this is a bit
of a simplification,

00:27:46.137 --> 00:27:47.970
or oversimplification,
of the amount of work

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:49.320
it takes to do all this.

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:52.150
But, if you start to
extrapolate these steps out,

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:54.720
she's been able to
work at her own pace

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:57.690
without having to worry about
trying to find who owns what,

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.930
who has access to what she
has access to, because we've

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.000
built this data catalog.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:06.180
We've built this
approach to metadata.

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:08.880
Over time, we're kind of
getting to this point where

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.280
we can start building
again toward self-service.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:14.160
So, just like we have a
SQL-- a great SQL interface

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.440
for querying that
data catalog, you

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:19.450
might want to continue down
the self-service path and say,

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:21.450
can we make these things
even more discoverable?

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:23.730
Can we put like a CRED
API on top of this?

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:27.860
So, one option is to take a
small application or small CRED

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:31.140
API that sits in front of
this BigQuery metadata catalog

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:33.240
and deploy that in, like,
Compute Engine, or App

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.030
Engine, or Container
Engine, and front

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:36.927
it with, like, cloud endpoints.

00:28:36.927 --> 00:28:38.510
The reason you might
go down this road

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:41.390
is particularly around
good API management,

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:44.750
and building-- again,
consistent and clear access.

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:47.510
Because the best way you can
enable sort of self-service

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:49.760
is, obviously, giving
everybody access to it,

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.300
but also giving
everybody access to

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:54.050
in a way that is most
beneficial to them.

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:56.510
So, if you've got a lot of
folks who are very API-driven,

00:28:56.510 --> 00:28:58.051
or want to build
new applications all

00:28:58.051 --> 00:29:00.800
the time, having an API
endpoint that they can

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.080
hit to learn more
about the data catalog

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:07.089
is very, very beneficial.

00:29:07.089 --> 00:29:08.630
And over time, you
can actually start

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.340
to extrapolate this
step even further,

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:13.430
and start fronting all of
the data sources you've got.

00:29:13.430 --> 00:29:16.564
So, not only do you have a CRED
API in front of the catalog,

00:29:16.564 --> 00:29:18.230
but you might actually
have one in front

00:29:18.230 --> 00:29:20.210
of every single dataset.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.060
So now, again, you're enabling
this further, you know,

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.140
deeper level of access to data.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:26.900
And this might be a
little bit overkill

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:28.820
for the team of four
people, but imagine

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:32.120
if that was a team
of 400 or 500 people.

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:34.220
As you think about
this kind of approach

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.370
permeating the
entire organization,

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:39.130
everyone wants to have
access, and you've

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:41.480
got to start building these
things for scale over time.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.780
So, picking the right tools
up front lets you adopt that,

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.390
and again, lets new teams pick
this approach up, as well.

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.710
Before we finish this,
I do want to talk

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:55.680
a little bit about security,
early security and identity,

00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:58.010
in kind of a broad sense.

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:00.360
We've got a great set of
identity and access management

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:02.520
tools called Cloud IAM.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:03.990
There's a ton of
sessions about it

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:05.050
throughout the next
couple of days,

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.380
so I urge you guys, if you're
interested to go dig into it.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:09.046
What I really want
to cover here though,

00:30:09.046 --> 00:30:12.027
is this idea that there's
policy inheritance that

00:30:12.027 --> 00:30:13.860
goes from the top all
the way to the bottom.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:17.400
That means, at any level, if
you set a policy on data access

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:19.716
or control, it does
filter all the way down.

00:30:19.716 --> 00:30:21.090
So, if you've got
an organization

00:30:21.090 --> 00:30:23.040
or a single project
that's kind of the host

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:26.490
project in your project
account with GCP,

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:28.650
and you've got individual
projects for maybe dev

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:32.640
staging, Q&amp;A, that sort
of thing, or production,

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:34.380
and then you have
individual resources

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.210
underneath those
projects, you can actually

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.610
control who has access to
what, as long as you've set

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:39.902
your organization up correctly.

00:30:39.902 --> 00:30:41.318
I'm not going to
go too deep here,

00:30:41.318 --> 00:30:43.140
but, basically, the
idea is to walk away

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:46.552
with is, we have the ability
to control who can see what,

00:30:46.552 --> 00:30:48.510
and who can pull things
down, and that's really

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:51.210
what you want to control.

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:53.580
And, for example, if you dig
into BigQuery a little bit,

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:56.220
you've got a handful of roles
and different responsibilities,

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:57.660
or different access
controls that they've

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:58.680
got based on that stuff.

00:31:04.780 --> 00:31:06.364
So, as you look at
what kind of what--

00:31:06.364 --> 00:31:08.030
if you look at and
think about what it's

00:31:08.030 --> 00:31:09.940
going to take to
build for the future,

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:13.060
I put the site up again, because
it's really important, if you

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:15.400
do nothing else, if
you build nothing else,

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:16.930
you have to get agreement.

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:18.490
That's the most important thing.

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.620
If you can't adopt
this idea that we

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:23.740
have to have consistency
around data sources, data

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:25.330
workloads, and then
the tools we're

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:27.070
going to use to
work with that data,

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:29.380
this is going to be a long road.

00:31:29.380 --> 00:31:32.040
As much as I talk about the
products and the blue hexagons

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:34.660
and stuff, a lot of
this is a cultural shift

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:35.667
in an organization.

00:31:35.667 --> 00:31:37.000
It's kind of a lifestyle change.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:38.890
You have to get everybody
on the same page.

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:41.560
And part of doing that is
saying, can we add consistency?

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:44.050
Or can we make this a
consistent view of the world

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:45.400
that we can all agree upon?

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:47.980
And this might be a small
subset of what you end up with.

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:50.890
It could be a much, much larger
picture with a lot more pieces

00:31:50.890 --> 00:31:53.830
to it, but it's important
that everybody agrees, again,

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:56.310
what the data is going
to look like coming in,

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:58.330
what it's going to get
use for on the way out,

00:31:58.330 --> 00:31:59.955
and where it's going
to live while it's

00:31:59.955 --> 00:32:03.260
sitting in your infrastructure.

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:04.106
As you think about--

00:32:04.106 --> 00:32:06.230
as you kind of get through
that consistency piece--

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:07.789
and, you know,
that's a tough road,

00:32:07.789 --> 00:32:09.830
but once you get through
that, then the next step

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:12.320
is really getting around to
some building the catalog.

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:14.240
Can you catalog
all the data sets?

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:17.210
Do you know exactly what lives
throughout the organization?

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:19.557
Can you get everyone
to kind of pony up

00:32:19.557 --> 00:32:21.890
and say, all you have to do
is write this little snippet

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:24.110
of JSON, and we'll be
able to leave you alone

00:32:24.110 --> 00:32:25.460
for the next few days.

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:28.100
If you can build the catalog,
that's a great first step.

00:32:28.100 --> 00:32:29.030
Then the next thing
you want to do, is

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:31.113
make sure your teams have
access to the tools they

00:32:31.113 --> 00:32:33.170
need to do this work.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.270
And this is not just the
tools that are in GCP,

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:37.700
but it's also, like, the
API access, or the SQL

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:38.897
access to the datasets.

00:32:38.897 --> 00:32:39.980
Can they get those things?

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:43.070
Have you set up identity
and understanding security

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:45.036
correctly, so that
everybody has access?

00:32:45.036 --> 00:32:46.910
Because what you want
people to be able to do

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:49.370
is work on their own,
without having to go

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:50.522
bother anyone else.

00:32:50.522 --> 00:32:51.980
If they can do
everything they need

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:54.140
to do without interacting
with somebody else,

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:56.710
then when they do interact, and
they go have lunch together,

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:58.670
it's a lot friendlier
conversation, as

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:01.040
opposed to arguing about
who has access to data.

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:04.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

